    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's cool. it seems like a lot of those things were just passed through the islamic or chinese world to get to the west though. like with the math or the gunpowder. i was wondering if there were any major systems of law or complex governance structures from Africa or the Americas that actually shaped how we think about society now, or were those areas just not really part of that \"foundation\" for the modern world?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the Islamic and Chinese worlds did play a significant role in transmitting knowledge to Europe, African and American civilizations did contribute to the development of modern systems of law and governance.\n\nIn Africa, the ancient Egyptian and Zulu civilizations established complex kingdoms with sophisticated systems of governance, law, and architecture. Additionally, the Sundiata Empire in Mali, the Ashanti Empire in Ghana, and the Kingdom of Kongo in Central Africa all developed advanced systems of law, justice, and social organization. The Yoruba people of West Africa, for example, developed a complex system of divination and governance that emphasized spirituality and collective decision-making.\n\nIn the Americas, the Inca Empire in South America built a highly organized and efficient empire with a sophisticated system of governance, record-keeping, and road networks. The Aztec and Mayan civilizations also developed complex systems of governance, law, and architecture. These systems, while often unknown or overlooked in Western history, have had a lasting impact on the development of modern societies.\n\nIt's worth noting that many of these African and American systems of law and governance have been largely forgotten or marginalized in Western history, and their contributions have only been recognized in recent years.",
